# BLoC Test Guidelines for MovieAlike Project

## Overview
This document defines the standardized testing patterns for BLoC components in the MovieAlike Flutter application. These guidelines ensure consistent, maintainable, and comprehensive test coverage.

## Test Structure Pattern

### File Organization
```
test/presentation/{feature}/
├── {feature}_bloc_test.dart      # Main BLoC test file
└── {feature}_widget_test.dart   # Widget tests (if needed)

test/mocks/
├── fake_{entity}.dart           # Fake data generators
└── mock_{service}.dart          # Mock implementations
```

### Test File Structure
Every BLoC test file must follow this exact structure:

```dart
import 'package:bloc_test/bloc_test.dart';
import 'package:flutter_test/flutter_test.dart';
import 'package:mocktail/mocktail.dart';
import 'package:moviealike/data/network_client/request_error.dart';
import 'package:moviealike/domain/{feature}/models/{entity}.dart';
import 'package:moviealike/domain/{feature}/use_cases/{use_case}.dart';
import 'package:moviealike/presentation/screens/{feature}/{feature}_bloc.dart';
import 'package:moviealike/presentation/screens/{feature}/{feature}_state.dart';
import 'package:result_type/result_type.dart';

import '../../mocks/fake_{entity}.dart';

class Mock{UseCase} extends Mock implements {UseCase} {
  static Mock{UseCase} dummy({List<{Entity}>? {entities}}) {
    final mock = Mock{UseCase}();
    when(() => mock.call(any())).thenAnswer(
      (_) async => Success({entities} ?? const []),
    );
    return mock;
  }
}

void main() {
  registerFallbackValue(getFake{Entity}());
  
  {Feature}Bloc get{Feature}Bloc({
    {UseCase}? {useCase},
    // ... other dependencies
  }) {
    return {Feature}Bloc(
      {useCase} ?? Mock{UseCase}.dummy(),
      // ... other dependencies
    );
  }

  group('{Feature}Bloc', () {
    group('{method_name}', () {
      group('when requests are successful', () {
        final {entities} = [
          getFake{Entity}(
            id: 1,
            title: 'Test Entity 1',
            // ... other properties
          ),
          getFake{Entity}(
            id: 2,
            title: 'Test Entity 2',
            // ... other properties
          ),
        ];
        final mock{UseCase} = Mock{UseCase}.dummy({entities}: {entities});
        final {feature}Bloc = get{Feature}Bloc({useCase}: mock{UseCase});

        blocTest<{Feature}Bloc, {Feature}State>(
          'emits correct state when use case returns success',
          build: () => {feature}Bloc,
          act: (bloc) => bloc.{method_name}(),
          expect: () => [
            // Loading state
            isA<{Feature}State>()
                .having((state) => state.isLoading, 'isLoading', isTrue)
                .having((state) => state.hasError, 'hasError', isFalse)
                .having((state) => state.error, 'error', isNull),
            // Success state
            isA<{Feature}State>()
                .having((state) => state.{entities}.length, '{entities} loaded', 2)
                .having((state) => state.isLoading, 'isLoading', isFalse)
                .having((state) => state.hasError, 'hasError', isFalse)
                .having((state) => state.error, 'error', isNull)
                .having((state) => state.{entities}.first.title, 'first {entity} title', 'Test Entity 1'),
          ],
        );
      });

      group('when use case fails', () {
        final mock{UseCase} = Mock{UseCase}();
        final error = ServerError(statusCode: 500);

        when(() => mock{UseCase}.call(any())).thenAnswer(
          (_) async => Failure(error),
        );

        final {feature}Bloc = get{Feature}Bloc({useCase}: mock{UseCase});

        blocTest<{Feature}Bloc, {Feature}State>(
          'emits error state when use case fails',
          build: () => {feature}Bloc,
          act: (bloc) => bloc.{method_name}(),
          expect: () => [
            // Loading state
            isA<{Feature}State>()
                .having((state) => state.isLoading, 'isLoading', isTrue)
                .having((state) => state.hasError, 'hasError', isFalse)
                .having((state) => state.error, 'error', isNull),
            // Error state
            isA<{Feature}State>()
                .having((state) => state.isLoading, 'isLoading', isFalse)
                .having((state) => state.hasError, 'hasError', isTrue)
                .having((state) => state.error, 'error', isA<ServerError>()),
          ],
        );
      });
    });
  });
}
```

## Test Organization Rules

### 1. Group Structure
- ✅ **MUST** use nested `group()` calls for logical organization
- ✅ **MUST** have main group for BLoC class name: `group('{Feature}Bloc', () {})`
- ✅ **MUST** have method groups: `group('{method_name}', () {})`
- ✅ **MUST** have scenario groups: `group('when requests are successful', () {})`
- ✅ **MUST** use descriptive group names that explain the test scenario

### 2. Mock and BLoC Setup
```dart
group('when requests are successful', () {
  final {entities} = [
    getFake{Entity}(id: 1, title: 'Test Entity 1'),
    getFake{Entity}(id: 2, title: 'Test Entity 2'),
  ];
  final mock{UseCase} = Mock{UseCase}.dummy({entities}: {entities});
  final {feature}Bloc = get{Feature}Bloc({useCase}: mock{UseCase});
});
```

**Rules:**
- ✅ **MUST** create test data first, then mocks, then BLoC
- ✅ **MUST** use `final` for all declarations
- ✅ **MUST** use helper functions for BLoC creation
- ✅ **MUST** use dummy mock methods for default behavior
- ✅ **MUST** create new instances for each scenario group
- ❌ **NEVER** share mocks or BLoCs between different scenario groups

### 3. Test Data Setup
```dart
group('when requests are successful', () {
  final {entities} = [
    getFake{Entity}(
      id: 1,
      title: 'Test Entity 1',
      overview: 'Overview 1',
    ),
    getFake{Entity}(
      id: 2,
      title: 'Test Entity 2',
      overview: 'Overview 2',
    ),
  ];
  final mock{UseCase} = Mock{UseCase}.dummy({entities}: {entities});
  final {feature}Bloc = get{Feature}Bloc({useCase}: mock{UseCase});
```

**Rules:**
- ✅ **MUST** define test data first at the group level
- ✅ **MUST** use `final` for test data variables (not `late`)
- ✅ **MUST** use `getFake{Entity}()` helper functions
- ✅ **MUST** provide meaningful test data with specific values
- ✅ **MUST** use dummy mock methods for default behavior
- ✅ **MUST** use helper functions for BLoC creation
- ❌ **NEVER** create test data inside `blocTest` build method
- ❌ **NEVER** use hardcoded entity creation in tests
- ❌ **NEVER** use `late` keyword for test data

### 4. BLoC Test Structure
```dart
blocTest<{Feature}Bloc, {Feature}State>(
  'descriptive test name',
  build: () => {feature}Bloc,
  act: (bloc) => bloc.{method_name}(),
  expect: () => [
    // State expectations
  ],
);
```

**Rules:**
- ✅ **MUST** use `build: () => {feature}Bloc` (no custom build logic)
- ✅ **MUST** use descriptive test names
- ✅ **MUST** test both loading and final states
- ✅ **MUST** use `isA<{Feature}State>()` for type checking
- ✅ **MUST** use `.having()` for specific property assertions
- ❌ **NEVER** put mock setup in `build` method

## State Testing Patterns

### 1. Loading State Pattern
```dart
isA<{Feature}State>()
    .having((state) => state.isLoading, 'isLoading', isTrue)
    .having((state) => state.hasError, 'hasError', isFalse)
    .having((state) => state.error, 'error', isNull),
```

### 2. Success State Pattern
```dart
isA<{Feature}State>()
    .having((state) => state.{entities}.length, '{entities} loaded', expectedCount)
    .having((state) => state.isLoading, 'isLoading', isFalse)
    .having((state) => state.hasError, 'hasError', isFalse)
    .having((state) => state.error, 'error', isNull)
    .having((state) => state.{entities}.first.title, 'first {entity} title', 'Expected Title'),
```

### 3. Error State Pattern
```dart
isA<{Feature}State>()
    .having((state) => state.isLoading, 'isLoading', isFalse)
    .having((state) => state.hasError, 'hasError', isTrue)
    .having((state) => state.error, 'error', isA<{ErrorType}>()),
```

## Mock Setup Rules

### 1. Mock Class Definition
```dart
class Mock{UseCase} extends Mock implements {UseCase} {
  static Mock{UseCase} dummy({List<{Entity}>? {entities}}) {
    final mock = Mock{UseCase}();
    when(() => mock.call(any())).thenAnswer(
      (_) async => Success({entities} ?? const []),
    );
    return mock;
  }
}
```

**Rules:**
- ✅ **MUST** use `Mock` from mocktail package
- ✅ **MUST** implement the actual use case interface
- ✅ **MUST** place mock classes at the top of the file
- ✅ **MUST** use descriptive names: `GetPopularMoviesMock`
- ✅ **MUST** include static `dummy()` method for default behavior
- ✅ **MUST** use `any()` matcher for parameters
- ✅ **MUST** provide default empty data for dummy methods

### 2. Mock Configuration
```dart
// For success scenarios - use dummy method
final mock{UseCase} = Mock{UseCase}.dummy({entities}: {entities});

// For error scenarios - override with when()
final mock{UseCase} = Mock{UseCase}();
when(() => mock{UseCase}.call(any())).thenAnswer(
  (_) async => Failure(error),
);
```

**Rules:**
- ✅ **MUST** use dummy methods for success scenarios
- ✅ **MUST** use `when().thenAnswer()` for error scenarios
- ✅ **MUST** wrap return values in `Success()` or `Failure()`
- ✅ **MUST** use meaningful test data
- ✅ **MUST** setup mocks at the group level
- ❌ **NEVER** setup mocks inside `blocTest` build method

## Helper Functions and Fallback Values

### 1. Fallback Value Registration
```dart
void main() {
  registerFallbackValue(getFake{Entity}());
  registerFallbackValue(SearchType.movie);
  registerFallbackValue(SearchFilter.genres);
  // ... other fallback values
}
```

**Rules:**
- ✅ **MUST** register fallback values for all complex types used with `any()` matcher
- ✅ **MUST** use fake data generators for model fallback values
- ✅ **MUST** register enum values directly
- ✅ **MUST** place registration at the top of `main()` function
- ❌ **NEVER** forget to register fallback values for custom types

### 2. BLoC Helper Functions
```dart
{Feature}Bloc get{Feature}Bloc({
  {UseCase}? {useCase},
  // ... other dependencies
}) {
  return {Feature}Bloc(
    {useCase} ?? Mock{UseCase}.dummy(),
    // ... other dependencies with defaults
  );
}
```

**Rules:**
- ✅ **MUST** create helper functions for BLoC instantiation
- ✅ **MUST** provide default mock instances for all dependencies
- ✅ **MUST** use dummy methods for default behavior
- ✅ **MUST** make all parameters optional
- ✅ **MUST** use descriptive function names: `getMovieDetailsBloc`
- ❌ **NEVER** create BLoCs directly in tests
- ❌ **NEVER** forget to provide defaults for all dependencies

## Test Scenarios Coverage

### 1. Success Scenarios
- ✅ **MUST** test successful data loading
- ✅ **MUST** verify correct state transitions (loading → success)
- ✅ **MUST** verify data integrity in final state
- ✅ **MUST** test with multiple data items

### 2. Error Scenarios
- ✅ **MUST** test single use case failure
- ✅ **MUST** test multiple use case failures
- ✅ **MUST** test different error types (ServerError, ConnectionError, etc.)
- ✅ **MUST** verify error state properties

### 3. Edge Cases
- ✅ **MUST** test empty data scenarios
- ✅ **MUST** test null/undefined scenarios
- ✅ **MUST** test timeout scenarios (if applicable)

## Fake Data Rules

### 1. Fake Data Generator Pattern
```dart
// File: test/mocks/fake_{entity}.dart
import 'dart:math';
import 'package:faker/faker.dart';
import 'package:moviealike/domain/{feature}/models/{entity}.dart';

{Entity} getFake{Entity}({
  int? id,
  String? title,
  String? overview,
  // ... other properties
}) =>
    {Entity}(
      id: id ?? faker.randomGenerator.integer(1000000),
      title: title ?? faker.lorem.sentence(),
      overview: overview ?? faker.lorem.sentence(),
      // ... other properties with faker defaults
    );
```

**Rules:**
- ✅ **MUST** use `faker` package for random data generation
- ✅ **MUST** provide optional parameters for specific test data
- ✅ **MUST** use meaningful default values
- ✅ **MUST** follow naming convention: `getFake{Entity}`
- ✅ **MUST** place in `test/mocks/` directory
- ❌ **NEVER** hardcode all values in fake generators

### 2. Fake Data Usage in Tests
```dart
final {entities} = [
  getFake{Entity}(
    id: 1,
    title: 'Specific Test Title',
    overview: 'Specific Test Overview',
  ),
  getFake{Entity}(
    id: 2,
    title: 'Another Test Title',
    overview: 'Another Test Overview',
  ),
];
```

**Rules:**
- ✅ **MUST** override specific properties for test scenarios
- ✅ **MUST** use meaningful test values
- ✅ **MUST** create multiple items for list testing
- ✅ **MUST** use consistent naming patterns

## Error Testing Patterns

### 1. Error Type Testing
```dart
// Server errors
final error = ServerError(statusCode: 500);
expect: () => [
  // ... loading state
  isA<{Feature}State>()
      .having((state) => state.error, 'error', isA<ServerError>()),
];

// Connection errors
final error = ConnectionError();
expect: () => [
  // ... loading state
  isA<{Feature}State>()
      .having((state) => state.error, 'error', isA<ConnectionError>()),
];
```

### 2. Multiple Error Scenarios
```dart
blocTest<{Feature}Bloc, {Feature}State>(
  'emits error state when both use cases fail',
  build: () {
    final firstError = ServerError(statusCode: 500);
    final secondError = ConnectionError();

    when(() => mockFirstUseCase()).thenAnswer(
      (_) async => Failure(firstError),
    );
    when(() => mockSecondUseCase()).thenAnswer(
      (_) async => Failure(secondError),
    );

    return {feature}Bloc;
  },
  // ... rest of test
);
```

## Test Naming Conventions

### 1. Group Names
- Main BLoC: `group('{Feature}Bloc', () {})`
- Methods: `group('{method_name}', () {})`
- Scenarios: `group('when requests are successful', () {})`
- Error scenarios: `group('when requests fail', () {})`

### 2. Test Names
- Success: `'emits correct state when use case returns success'`
- Error: `'emits error state when use case fails'`
- Specific: `'emits error state when getPopularMovies fails'`
- Multiple: `'emits error state when both use cases fail'`

### 3. Property Names in Assertions
- Use descriptive names: `'isLoading'`, `'hasError'`, `'error'`
- Use data-specific names: `'populaMovies loaded'`, `'upcomingMovies loaded'`
- Use specific item names: `'first popular movie title'`

